EXT. DESERT RAIL HALT - TELEGRAPH OFFICE - LATE AFTERNOON

A single wooden shack beside a single rail line, a thousand miles from anywhere. The platform boards are bleached silver. Sage grass trembles in a hot wind. The horizon is a thin blade of light. A windmill creaks over a stock tank.

Inside the shack, a dusty window throws a bar of gold across a roll-top desk. A telegraph key sits at its centre like a small brass insect, silent. A clock ticks on the wall.

EZRA CALLOWAY, 60s, stationmaster, sleeves held up by arm garters, sits with his boots on the desk reading a seed catalogue. MARISOL VEGA, 30s, a widow in a travelling dress the colour of dust, sits upright on the waiting bench with a carpetbag on her knees. She has been there since morning.

The clock ticks. Ezra turns a page. Marisol watches a fly crawl across the window.

EZRA
Train's due at four.

MARISOL
It's ten past.

EZRA
It'll come.

MARISOL
You said that at noon.

EZRA
I did.

Another page. The fly lifts off, whirs, settles on the key.

EZRA (CONT'D)
You headed far?

MARISOL
Far enough.

EZRA
West?

MARISOL
West.

EZRA
There's nothing west.

MARISOL
Then it'll be quiet.

Ezra smiles into his catalogue. She smoothes her skirt. Her fingers find a small tin locket at her throat. She holds it, lets go.

EZRA
My wife used to say a person only goes west to forget.

MARISOL
Did she forget?

EZRA
She went east.

A faint sound. A rumble, far down the line, no louder than a stomach. Ezra lowers his boots. Marisol stands.

HAWTHORNE (O.S.)
(calling from the platform)
Smoke on the horizon, Ezra!

Ezra goes to the door. Squints. Comes back.

EZRA
That's her.

MARISOL
Is she on time?

EZRA
An hour late.

MARISOL
Good.

EZRA
Good?

MARISOL
I wasn't ready.

She picks up her bag. Sets it down again. Ezra looks at her. Sees her. Doesn't say anything.

The rumble grows. The rails begin to sing. The windmill turns faster. Through the window, a smudge of black smoke climbs the sky.

HAWTHORNE (O.S.)
(closer)
Two minutes!

EZRA
You've got a ticket?

MARISOL
Bought it in Fort Aurelius.

EZRA
West of here?

MARISOL
East.

EZRA
Then you're going the wrong way.

MARISOL
I know.

She smiles a small, tired smile. Ezra nods. Takes his hat from a peg. He takes her carpetbag, without asking, and carries it to the door.

EZRA
The platform's uneven. Mind the third board.

MARISOL
Thank you, Mr. Calloway.

EZRA
Ezra.

MARISOL
Ezra.

They step out into the light. The rumble becomes a roar. Wind pulls at Marisol's hair. She holds the locket against her throat.

HAWTHORNE (O.S.)
(shouting over the engine)
She's coming in hot!

The locomotive thunders into frame, steam and iron and sun. Ezra holds Marisol's bag out of the dust.

The brakes scream. The train stops. Silence, and the hiss of steam. Ezra hands up the bag. Marisol climbs the step, turns once.

MARISOL
Ezra.

EZRA
Ma'am.

MARISOL
Plant the tomatoes in May.

He touches his hat. The conductor's whistle sounds. The train begins to move.

Ezra watches until it is a smudge again, then a line, then nothing. He walks back into the office, sits, and takes up the seed catalogue.

The key on the desk begins to chatter.
